\section{Symplectic Control Operators}

TSSE requires that control operators be symplectic:
\[
\mathcal{C}(t)^\top J\,\mathcal{C}(t) = J.
\]

\begin{definition}[Symplectic Control Operator]
A control operator $\mathcal{C}(t)$ is symplectic if it preserves the canonical
symplectic form.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Symplectic Control Preservation]
If $\mathcal{C}(t)$ is symplectic, then:
\[
\det(D\mathcal{C}(t)) = 1.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Symplectic matrices have unit determinant.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Invariant-Preserving Control]
If $\mathcal{C}(t)$ is symplectic and Hamiltonian-consistent, then it preserves all
TSSE invariants.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Symplecticity preserves canonical structure; Hamiltonian consistency preserves
energy; temporal symmetry enforcement preserves $\mathcal{T}$ invariants.
\end{proof}

This theorem establishes the invariant-preserving nature of TSSE control.

