\section{Autonomous Manifold Correction}

Let $\delta_{\mathcal{S}}(t)$ denote the manifold deviation metric:
\[
\delta_{\mathcal{S}}(t) =
\text{dist}\left(
\mathcal{U}(t)(q,p),\;
\mathcal{S}_{\text{TSSE–AITFE}}
\right).
\]

Define the autonomous correction operator:
\[
\mathcal{C}_{\mathcal{S}}(t) : (q,p) \mapsto (q,p),
\]
which satisfies:
\[
\mathcal{C}_{\mathcal{S}}(t)(q,p) \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{TSSE–AITFE}}.
\]

\begin{lemma}[Manifold Correction]
If $\delta_{\mathcal{S}}(t) > 0$, then:
\[
\mathcal{C}_{\mathcal{S}}(t)(q,p)
=
\arg\min_{(q',p') \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{TSSE–AITFE}}}
\text{dist}\left((q,p),(q',p')\right).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Correction is defined as projection onto the manifold.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Autonomous Manifold Stability]
If $\mathcal{C}_{\mathcal{S}}(t)$ is symplectic and Hamiltonian-consistent, then:
\[
\delta_{\mathcal{S}}(t) = 0.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Symplectic projection preserves canonical structure; Hamiltonian consistency ensures
energy preservation; manifold projection ensures closure.
\end{proof}

This theorem establishes autonomous manifold stability.

